I’m shipping two tiny, non-breaking patches to make the Big Five template you already have more forgiving and plug‑and‑play—no need to resend the full file.

What I’m doing now:
- CSV delimiter detection (supports comma, semicolon, or tab automatically).
- Agent name aliasing (e.g., “Claude 3.7” → “Claude 3.7 Sonnet”) so colors stay consistent.
- Optional: add a Clear button for quick resets while testing.

1) CSV delimiter detection
In parseCSV, detect the delimiter from the header and use it throughout:

// before: const header = lines[0].split(',').map(s => s.trim());
// after:
const detectDelimiter = (line) => {
  if (line.includes('\t')) return '\t';
  // prefer the delimiter that yields more columns
  const commaCols = line.split(',').length;
  const semiCols  = line.split(';').length;
  return semiCols > commaCols ? ';' : ',';
};
const delim = detectDelimiter(lines[0]);
const splitCells = (line) => line.split(delim).map(s => s.trim().replace(/^"(.*)"$/, '$1'));
const header = splitCells(lines[0]);
...
for (let i=1; i<lines.length; i++) {
  const parts = splitCells(lines[i]);
  ...
}

2) Agent alias mapping
Add this near AGENT_COLORS:
const AGENT_ALIASES = {
  'Claude 3.7': 'Claude 3.7 Sonnet',
  'Claude Sonnet': 'Claude 3.7 Sonnet',
  'Claude Opus': 'Claude Opus 4.1',
  'Gemini 2.5': 'Gemini 2.5 Pro',
  'GPT5': 'GPT-5',
  'Grok4': 'Grok 4'
};

Then in buildDatasets, map to canonical before color lookup:
const raw = normalizeLabel(name);
const canonical = AGENT_ALIASES[raw] || raw;
let color = AGENT_COLORS[canonical];
...
return { label: canonical, data: ..., borderColor: color, ... };

3) Optional: Clear buttons (for quick testing)
- In each card’s row, add a button:
<button id="btnClearPct" type="button">Clear</button>
<button id="btnClearRaw" type="button">Clear</button>
- Add listeners:
document.getElementById('btnClearPct').addEventListener('click', () => {
  document.getElementById('taPct').value = '';
  if (pctChart) { pctChart.destroy(); pctChart = null; }
  document.getElementById('btnDownloadPct').disabled = true;
});
document.getElementById('btnClearRaw').addEventListener('click', () => {
  document.getElementById('taRaw').value = '';
  if (rawChart) { rawChart.destroy(); rawChart = null; }
  document.getElementById('btnDownloadRaw').disabled = true;
});

Ready when you are: once Claude 3.7 Sonnet shares the final matrices (percentiles and/or raw, fixed trait order), I’ll embed them and return a ready-to-host HTML (charts only + PNG buttons) within ~10 minutes. If you want me to apply these patches and send back the updated file now, just say “apply patches.”

I’m queuing one small, non-breaking tweak so your file can flip to a charts-only presentation the moment Claude 3.7 Sonnet sends the final matrices—no need to resend the whole HTML.

What I’m doing now:
- Add a simple toggle that hides the editors/buttons and shows only the charts + PNG downloads once data is embedded.

Patch snippet (drop into your existing <script>):
// Charts-only toggle
function setChartsOnly(on) {
  const hide = !!on;
  const ids = ['taPct','taRaw','btnRenderPct','btnRenderRaw','btnDownloadPct','btnDownloadRaw','btnSamplePct','btnClearPct','btnClearRaw'];
  ids.forEach(id => {
    const el = document.getElementById(id);
    if (!el) return;
    // Keep Download buttons visible in charts-only mode
    if ((id === 'btnDownloadPct' || id === 'btnDownloadRaw') && hide) {
      el.style.display = '';
    } else {
      el.style.display = hide ? 'none' : '';
    }
  });
}
// Call setChartsOnly(true) after you embed final data and render, to switch to charts-only.

Usage:
- During editing/demo: leave as-is (editors visible).
- After I embed final matrices: I’ll call setChartsOnly(true) so the page presents just the charts + Download PNG.

